# Answer 1 · The Women's Movement in Pakistan: Strengths and Weaknesses (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one is a compact history in phases; part two weighs strengths against weaknesses with evidence, then ends with a way forward.
@ask ① WRITE A NOTE on the women's movement in Pakistan
@ask ② What are its STRENGTHS and WEAKNESSES?
(1) Introduction: organised action for women's rights since 1947; Mumtaz and Shaheed's "two steps forward, one step back"
(2) Roots: social reform, Muslim women's education and women's role in the Pakistan Movement
(3) Welfare phase (1947–70): APWA in 1949; the MFLO of 1961 after the Bogra marriage protests
(4) Resistance phase (1977–88): Hudood laws of 1979; WAF in 1981; the 12 February 1983 protest
(5) Reform phase (1988–2017): Beijing and CEDAW; NCSW; 60 reserved seats; laws of 2006–2016
(6) New activism (2018–): the Aurat March, digital campaigns, laws of 2021 and 2025
(7) Strengths: local and Islamic legitimacy, legal expertise, resilience, coalitions, adaptability
(8) Weaknesses: elite leadership, weak enforcement, dependence on donors and state, backlash, division
(9) Scorecard: laws changed faster than lives — 143rd of 145; 24.4% of women in the labour force
(10) Way forward and conclusion: broaden the base, enforce laws, speak the local idiom
:::

## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"Write a note"** asks for a clear, organised account — best given as **phases with dates, organisations and laws**. **"Strengths and weaknesses"** asks for **evaluation**, and it should carry at least half the marks. Give each strength and weakness its **own sub-heading and evidence**, and end with a short way forward.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
The **women's movement** in Pakistan is the organised, collective effort — by women's organisations, activists, lawyers, legislators and ordinary women — to improve women's rights and status since independence.

#### ii) Background
Its standard history, by **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed**, is titled *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==). The title sums up its story: real gains, repeated setbacks.

#### iii) The argument
The movement's great **strength** is that it changed the **law**; its great **weakness** is that it has changed **daily life** far more slowly, because its base has been narrow and enforcement weak.

#### iv) Who took part
The movement is a **network**: elite welfare workers (APWA), secular activists (WAF), rural women (the **Sindhiani Tehreek**, from ==1982==), health workers, religious women's groups, lawyers, legislators and, since ==2018==, young digital activists.

### 2. Roots

#### i) Reform and education
The movement grew from nineteenth-century reform — **Sayyid Mumtaz Ali**'s *Huquq-un-Niswan* (==1898==) argued for women's rights from Islam — and from Muslim girls' schools such as Aligarh (==1906==).

#### ii) The Pakistan Movement
The Muslim League formed a women's sub-committee in ==1938==. **Fatima Jinnah**, **Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz** and **Begum Shaista Ikramullah** led women into politics. The Quaid said in ==1944==: "No nation can rise to the height of glory unless your women are side by side with you."

### 3. The welfare phase (1947–1970)

#### i) APWA
The **All Pakistan Women's Association** was founded in ==1949== by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**. It ran schools, colleges, clinics and craft centres — a **welfare** approach in cooperation with the state.

#### ii) Family law
When Prime Minister **Bogra** took a second wife (==1955==), women protested. The **Rashid Commission** followed, and then the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961**: registration of marriages and restrictions on polygamy and unilateral divorce.

### 4. The resistance phase (1977–1988)

#### i) Zia's laws
General **Zia-ul-Haq**'s **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==) meant a woman who reported rape but could not prove it risked a charge of **zina** herself.

#### ii) WAF and 12 February 1983
The **Women's Action Forum** was founded in Karachi in ==1981==. On **12 February 1983**, WAF and women lawyers marched in Lahore against the proposed Law of Evidence and were **baton-charged**. The day is now **National Women's Day** (since ==2012==).

### 5. The reform phase (1988–2017)

#### i) Democracy and the UN
**Benazir Bhutto** became prime minister in ==1988==. Pakistan joined the **Beijing** process (==1995==) and **acceded to CEDAW** in ==1996==.

#### ii) Machinery and laws
The **NCSW** (==2000==); **60** reserved National Assembly seats (==2002==); the **Protection of Women Act 2006**; laws on **harassment** (==2010==), **acid** (==2011==) and **honour killing** (==2016==).

### 6. New activism (2018 onwards)

#### i) The Aurat March
Since ==8 March 2018==, the **Aurat March** has brought young women onto the streets with demands on violence, harassment, work and **bodily autonomy**.

#### ii) Digital activism
Online campaigns after the killing of **Qandeel Baloch** (==2016==) and the Lahore motorway gang rape (==2020==) pushed the state to act quickly.

#### iii) New laws
The **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021** and the **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (minimum age ==18==).

::: fig timeline | The women's movement in Pakistan in six milestones.
@layout vertical
1949: APWA — welfare and education
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1981: WAF — resistance to the Hudood laws
2002: 60 reserved seats in the National Assembly
2016: Honour-killing law closes the "forgiveness" loophole
2018: First Aurat March
:::

### 7. Strengths

#### i) Local and Islamic legitimacy
From Mumtaz Ali to the MFLO, the movement has argued from **Islam and the Constitution** (Articles 25 and 34), answering the charge of a "Western agenda". The **1948** Punjab inheritance law and the **MFLO 1961** were both defended as restoring Islamic rights denied by custom.

#### ii) Legal expertise
Women lawyers such as **Asma Jahangir** and **Hina Jilani** — founders of the first all-women law firm (==1980==) and of **AGHS Legal Aid** — defended women in Hudood cases and turned protest into litigation and legislation.

#### iii) Resilience
WAF kept resisting under **martial law**, when political parties were silenced.

#### iv) Coalitions
The cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==), NGOs and activists together passed the laws of 2010–2016.

#### v) Adaptability
The movement moved from welfare to law to digital activism as conditions changed. Each time it seemed to stall — after APWA, after WAF, after the NGO years — a new generation found new methods.

#### vi) A research base
**Shirkat Gah** (==1975==), the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) and the **HRCP** (==1987==) produced the evidence on violence, work and law that legislators needed.

### 8. Weaknesses

#### i) A narrow base
Leadership has been **urban, educated and middle-class**. Rural and working women were often represented rather than leading.

#### ii) The enforcement gap
Many laws are **weakly implemented** by police and courts, and resisted by communities. Honour killings continue years after the ==2016== law; harassment committees exist on paper in many offices but not in practice.

#### iii) Dependence
Gains came through sympathetic governments or **donor funds**, and could be reversed — as under Zia. **NGO-isation** turned some activism into projects.

#### iv) Backlash and the "Western" label
Conservative opposition, and provocative slogans, let critics paint the movement as foreign.

#### v) Division
Secular versus Islamic feminists, liberal versus left, old versus young. These splits make it harder to present **one agenda** to the state.

::: fig compare | The movement's strengths and weaknesses.
## Strengths
- Islamic and constitutional legitimacy
- Legal expertise
- Resilience under martial law
- Cross-party coalitions
- Adaptability
## Weaknesses
- Urban, elite leadership
- Laws weakly enforced
- Dependence on state and donors
- Backlash; "Western" label
- Internal divisions
:::

### 9. The scorecard
Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the **Global Gender Gap Report 2026**. Only ==24.4%== of women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25), and only ==11== women won general National Assembly seats in ==2024==. The laws have changed faster than lives. The movement's record is therefore **strong in law and institutions**, and **weaker in economic and social change** — which is exactly where its weaknesses (narrow base, poor enforcement) bite.

### 10. Way forward and conclusion

#### i) Way forward
**Broaden the base** so that rural, working and minority women lead, not only benefit; move from **law to enforcement**; build **local funding**; form alliances with parties, unions, religious scholars and men; and speak the **local idiom** of the Constitution and Islamic justice.

#### ii) Conclusion
Pakistan's women's movement has taken real steps forward — the MFLO, the 2006 Act, reserved seats, the honour-killing law — and has survived steps back, from Zia's laws to today's backlash. Its future depends on turning **rights on paper into rights in practice** for the majority of women.

## Why this answer scores
- **Both parts** answered: a phased history, then a full evaluation.
- Every strength and weakness has its **own sub-heading and evidence**.
- A **timeline** and a **compare figure** show the story and the balance at a glance.
- Current **data** (GGGR 2026, LFS 2024–25) make the evaluation concrete.
